---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../../styles/global.css";
---
<Layout
  title="Hanfu Etiquette: Rules and Manners"
  description="Hanfu etiquette guide: collar direction, how to bow, dress rules for occasions, sitting, walking, and ritual behavior. Practical etiquette for wearing Hanfu respectfully in China and abroad."
  canonicalUrl="https://www.dresses-hanfu.com/guides/etiquette/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Guides", url: "/guides" }, { name: "Hanfu Etiquette", url: "/guides/etiquette" }]}
  articleSchema={{
    datePublished: "2026-08-01",
    dateModified: "2026-08-08",
    author: { "@type": "Person", "name": "Hanfu Dress Guide Editorial Team" }
  }}
>
  <SiteHeader active="/guides/how-to-wear" />
  <main>
    <article>
      <section class="section" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <p style="font-size:0.8125rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-bottom:1rem;"><a href="/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/guides/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">Guides</a> &rsaquo; Hanfu Etiquette</p>
          <h1>Hanfu Etiquette: The Do's and Don'ts</h1>
          <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--color-muted);margin-top:1.5rem;line-height:1.55;">
            Wearing Hanfu is about more than the clothes. Historically, dress was inseparable from ritual — how you wrapped the collar, tied the sash, and walked mattered. Here is the practical etiquette every modern wearer should know, from collar direction to festival manners.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" style="padding-top:0;">
        <div class="container-narrow">
          <h2>1. The Collar Rule: Right Over Left</h2>
          <p class="text-muted">
            This is the non-negotiable rule. Hanfu collars cross <strong>right over left</strong> (left side of the fabric over the right... men do the same). The reverse, left over right, is how the dead are dressed for burial. If you get one thing right, get this.
          </p>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">2. Sash and Belt Placement</h2>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Tie the sash neatly at the natural waist (or at ribcage for Tang high-waisted styles) and tuck the ends or let them extend symmetrically. A sloppy, trailing, half-tied sash reads as careless. In formal wear, the belt's material and ornament followed rank; today, simply keep the knot tidy and consistent.
          </p>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">3. Choose Colors for the Occasion</h2>
          <p class="text-muted">
            Bright, saturated colors (red, gold, festival tones) suit celebrations and weddings. Subdued palettes (Song-era greys and blues) suit ceremonies, museums, tea events. Avoid wearing pure white to a wedding or victory-celebration event — in traditional color language, white reads as mourning. See our full <a href="/guides/colors/" style="color:var(--color-accent);">colors guide</a>.
          </p>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">4. Behavior in Traditional Spaces</h2>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem;">Everyday conduct</h3>
          <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
            <li><strong>Remove shoes</strong> before entering temples, tatami-style raised floor rooms, and traditional homes where the custom applies.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep the robe off the floor</strong> — long skirts drag; keep the hem lifted when climbing stairs.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem;">At ceremonies</h3>
          <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
            <li><strong>Respect ritual performance.</strong> If you're joining a traditional ceremony (like a coming-of-age or wedding ritual), follow the organizer's instructions on where to stand and when to bow.</li>
            <li><strong>Do bow modestly</strong> to elders or officiants if prompted. A slight namaste at the waist with hands folded is standard.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">5. Wearing Hanfu Respectfully Abroad</h2>
          <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem;">For international enthusiasts</h3>
          <p class="text-muted">
            The etiquette is straightforward: you're wearing a living traditional garment, not a costume. A few things to keep in mind:
          </p>
          <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;color:var(--color-muted);font-size:0.9375rem;line-height:1.8;">
            <li>Learn a little about the dynasty you're representing. People may ask.</li>
            <li>Keep the collar rule exact, especially in photos that could go public.</li>
            <li>There's no "gatekeeping" that says foreigners can't participate — the community is broadly welcoming. Do your basics right and you're a participant, not an intruder.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 style="margin-top:3rem;">6. A Note on Ceremony Etiquette</h2>
          <p class="text-muted">
            In traditional formal events with Hanfu — the coming-of-age ceremony (ji li / ji guan), weddings, or sacrificial rites — the dressing rules were strict and graded. For everyday wear, don't overthink ritual; the etiquette essentials are simply: right-over-left collar, tidy sash but not too tight, and sleeve hems kept off the floor. That covers 95% of daily situations.
          </p>

          <div style="margin-top:3rem;padding:2rem;background:var(--color-accent-light);border-radius:var(--radius-md);">
            <p style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:500;font-family:var(--font-display);">Continue Learning</p>
            <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:0.75rem;margin-top:1rem;">
              <a href="/guides/how-to-wear/" class="btn btn-outline">How to Wear Hanfu</a>
              <a href="/guides/meaning/" class="btn btn-outline">Hanfu Meaning</a>
              <a href="/guides/colors/" class="btn btn-outline">Hanfu Colors</a>
              <a href="/guides/hairstyles/" class="btn btn-outline">Hanfu Hairstyles</a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>